{"entity": "Circuit Analysis Fundamentals", "type": "course", "description": "Circuit Analysis Fundamentals is a foundational subject in electrical engineering education that encompasses the core theoretical principles, methodological approaches, and practical experimental techniques required to understand and predict the behavior of electrical circuits. It serves as the prerequisite theoretical course for the Circuit Analysis Fundamentals Laboratory course, bridging conceptual learning with hands-on experimentation. The subject emphasizes analysis of fundamental circuit elements—including resistors, ideal voltage sources, and RC networks—and their behavior under various configurations, particularly series and parallel arrangements. Key topics include the relationships among voltage, current, and resistance; application of network laws such as Kirchhoff’s laws and Ohm’s law; and the dynamic response of first-order circuits, especially how RC networks (e.g., low-pass filters) react to given inputs. Instruction is supported by schematic diagrams and figures—such as Figures 2.1 and 2.2—which illustrate circuit configurations and facilitate learning through visualization. Practical components involve measurement techniques using standard electrical instruments, enabling students to observe, verify, and interpret theoretical predictions in real-world contexts. Collectively, Circuit Analysis Fundamentals integrates theory, computation, schematic interpretation, and laboratory practice to establish a rigorous basis for advanced study and application in electrical engineering."}
